@ngoldbaum - I think the problem is at some level astropy's -- we have code that effectively wraps `np.common_dtype`, trying to be a bit more careful with specific string lengths, but for some historical reason we output a string rather than a dtype. Even without user dtypes, we cannot actually use `dtype.str`, since that does not correctly cover structured dtypes, so we have https://github.com/astropy/astropy/blob/711c366c09990896b710c45c851c4ce3b5c9bccc/astropy/utils/metadata/utils.py#L58-L62:
```
return (
        arr_common.dtype.str
        if arr_common.dtype.names is None
        else arr_common.dtype.descr
    )
```

I made a PR (https://github.com/astropy/astropy/pull/19199) that changes this to just return a `dtype` proper, which requires very little changes internally. So, I think in that respect we are fine.

That said, for things like the buffer protocol or the array API it might be good to have a standard way to describe user-defined formats, and I think your suggestion makes sense; at some level, one simply wants `eval(repr(dtype))` to work, and it does make sense to do this as part of the registration of the dtype.

