> Yeah, I bet we could add a new if statement there that makes PyArrayObject opaque on the opaque PyObject ABI. Hopefully close to no one is accessing e.g. ob_type.

Yeah, that sounds reasonable, the PEP is a bit sparse about it, but I would think `PyObject_GetTypeData()` probably already works if it returned a `PyArrayObjects_FieldsOnly` struct that omits the `PyObject *` part?
We *could* also add a convenience function for that to our API table or header (if that makes things easier).

I.e. I am thining that we do something like:
```
// PyArrayObject_fields will include PyObject when not opaque, but omit it otherwise.

static inline PyArrayObject_fields * PyArray_GetFields(PyArrayObject *arr) {
#ifdef NEW_STABLE_API
    // new public API table function or use PyObject_GetTypeData
    // if we need a public API function, will need NumPy version checks here also.
    return _PyArray_GetFields();
#else
    return (PyArrayObject_fields *)arr;
#endif
}

static inline size_t PyArray_NDIM(PyArrayObject *arr) {
    return PyArray_GetFields(arr)->ndim;
}
```
(Unlike `PyObject_GetTypeData()` we cannot return an error, I guess. The user has to pass a `PyArrayObject *`)

Which seems relatively doable and probably even helps us in theory convert at some point. But the priority here isn't converting NumPy (yet), but just enabling this, which seems like churn, but not too bad?

(Yeah, we'll have to make sure to replace every `PyArrayObject_fields *` cast in the public headers meticulously.)

I opened https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/30744 which makes it so that the fields after the PyObject header in the `PyArray_Descr` object are in a new fields struct and migrates NumPy's internals to use accessor macros everywhere.

It's a pretty big internal diff. Anyone trying to support the opaque PyObject ABI would need to do something similar if we want to make `PyArray_Descr` opaque in that build.

@seberg I'm curious what you think of my PR. Is it too much? Also consider we need to do the same thing for all the scalar type objects, the iterator objects, and a few others as I [described above](https://github.com/numpy/numpy/issues/30704#issuecomment-3781471618). Yes it's a change in the C API but also no one needs to target the NumPy 2.5 C API unless they want to opt into that.

@kumaraditya303 pointed out to me that static inline functions might have a performance penalty, especially on Windows because MSVC is more conservative about inlining static inline functions.

I like the static inline functions because they give type safety, but I don't think that's worth any performance overhead, especially if the overhead is unpredictable or platform-dependent. We're going to be paying function call overhead on the opaque PyObject build but we don't want to have that overhead elsewhere if we can avoid it.

To see if there's an issue, I think I might go ahead and migrate the ufunc object and then maybe run the ufunc benchmarks on Windows to see if there's any overhead? I'm not sure if there's a more realistic benchmarking approach I can use to see if there's any real-world performance impact.

