@devmt04 wrote

>  Since np.sign() does not explicitly handle NaT as a special case ...

Yes, and that is exactly the problem.  It *can* handle NaT as a special case, which is what I implemented in gh-29498.  This is consistent with the behavior for floating point values, where `np.sign(nan)` returns `nan`.

@WarrenWeckesser after thinking a lot about this case since you linked it to the one I raised, I personally believe that there are only 2 logical implementations for np.sign(timedelta).

Implementation 1:

```python
raise TypeError
```

Implementation 2:

```python
if x > 0:
    return np.timedelta64(1)
if x < 0:
    return np.timedelta64(-1)
return np.timedelta64(0)
```
I.e., the output would be still along the "time axis" (similar to how the sign of a complex number preserves angle, so it is important that the output is still in the "time" domain), however, the specific time _unit of measure_ would be discarded in favor of "generic time units". I think this would be the most mathematically accurate result if numpy chooses to give _any_ result other than an error.

@seberg absolute values are, mathematically speaking, not defined on units of measure at all (i.e. |m| is meaningless), but given numpy's current implementation of that operation for timedelta (which is: "|scalar*uom| = |scalar|*uom"), I think you're right: the sign method should probably return a value in the domain of the wrapped scalar (ℤ in this case).

(Only question I'd have would be... which "flavor" of ℤ? The built-in `int`? `int64` to match `timedelta64`? `int8` for the minimum size sufficient? Technically even `timedelta64` meets this requirement since it is masquerading as ℤ via its superclass `numbers.Integral`. And don't forget about `TypeError`, still a plausible candidate in my opinion, which also meets this requirement since `typing.Never` ⊂ ℤ 😆.

You also mentioned `NaT` which poses another wrinkle, also implying that `timedelta64` is definitely not in ℤ. Shouldn't the output be floating point then, unless you resign yourself to mixed-domain outputs?)

```
[numpy/numpy/_core/src/multiarray/convert_datatype.c](https://github.com/numpy/numpy/blob/50997e3c3b1f9331d3b03e3867c4896422278e52/numpy/_core/src/multiarray/convert_datatype.c#L1329-L1351)

Lines 1329 to 1351 in [50997e3](https://github.com/numpy/numpy/commit/50997e3c3b1f9331d3b03e3867c4896422278e52)

 case NPY_DOUBLE: { 
     double value = *(double *)valueptr; 
     if ((value > -65000 && value < 65000) || !npy_isfinite(value)) { 
         return NPY_HALF; 
     } 
     else if (value > -3.4e38 && value < 3.4e38) { 
         return NPY_FLOAT; 
     } 
     break; 
 } 
 case NPY_LONGDOUBLE: { 
     npy_longdouble value = *(npy_longdouble *)valueptr; 
     if ((value > -65000 && value < 65000) || !npy_isfinite(value)) { 
         return NPY_HALF; 
     } 
     else if (value > -3.4e38 && value < 3.4e38) { 
         return NPY_FLOAT; 
     } 
     else if (value > -1.7e308 && value < 1.7e308) { 
         return NPY_DOUBLE; 
     } 
     break; 
 } 
```
I think that this hardcoded approximation logic isn't correct in either case, since comparison done in double or longdouble space, values right at or near the float32 boundary can be misclassified depending on how the literal is rounded.
So I have a new proposal with the logic,
rather than trying to tune these numeric thresholds, I think a different approach based on representability instead of raw value ranges:

- For a given float scalar (float16/32/64/longdouble), we try candidate smaller float types in order.
- For each candidate type, we cast to that type and back, and check whether the original value can be recovered within that type’s precision.
- Only if the value round-trips (within an epsilon-based tolerance for float64/longdouble) do we demote to that smaller dtype.

I think changing the logic in above way would be better and give more accurate answer.

If I were to be assigned on this issue, I can implement it in the above way

