Glad I could help ;). I've looked up `__array_ufunc__` and that does seem like it could also work. The only thing I'm afraid of there is that (if I've understood correctly) the behavior would have to be implemented for every function with which the user could try and call the subclassed array. 

But taking the bottom up approach and looking at the C code I have the feeling that once a couple of functions like
```C
extern double c_abs(complex *);
extern void c_cos(complex *, complex *);
extern void c_div(complex *, complex *, complex *);
extern void c_exp(complex *, complex *);
extern void c_log(complex *, complex *);
extern void c_sin(complex *, complex *);
extern void c_sqrt(complex *, complex *);
extern void pow_ci(complex *, complex *, integer *);
extern void r_cnjg(complex *, complex *);
extern float r_imag(complex *);
```
have been defined for dual numbers then all other code will automatically follow. So it would seem to me that the bottom up approach is ultimately much more powerful since it will work in functions we have never anticipated.

And to play devils advocate: why should only complex numbers be recognized as fundamental number systems whereas dual numbers and split-complex numbers are discriminated against? It is true that these numbers are not always divisible, but matrices are also not always invertable but they are still pretty useful :). So I advocate for equal rights for all numbers of the form a + b i, whether i^2 = -1, i^2 = 0 or i^2 = 1.

