In 1.17, we got pocketfft for much faster FFTs, so that is probably the reason.

I profiled NumPy master, and it is not super obvious to me this is a large issue.  The example calls millions of very short FFTs (length 2), so the overhead of each of those FFT must be larger.  I.e. most time is spend in the `pass_all` function: https://github.com/numpy/numpy/blob/ec62be21cf109dac436f9a68a84fbd674c1faebb/numpy/fft/_pocketfft.c#L883

There are some allocations and probably other things which do not have to be repeated every time, moving them out could probably speed things up by 20+% at least (it is also using `malloc`).  Also just choosing the plan every time in `cfft_forward` might take a decent chunk of the total runtime.

In any case, I am unsure what the tradeoffs are. I do not think we have to try and get length 2 FFTs to the same level they were.  But making them slightly faster could be nice, and I do not know whether the difference is gone for 124 FFTs and we may care about those.

