If we implement an IEEE-754 totalOrder predicate in NumPy’s C-API (e.g. npy_total_order_<T>) alongside the existing </> predicates, it could let internal sort routines, ufuncs, and downstream libraries obtain the canonical IEEE-754-2008 ordering (negative NaNs before finite negatives, –∞…–0, +0…+∞, positive NaNs) with a single call. A proposed implementation lives in a new file numpy/core/src/common/npy_totorder.c (and headers in numpy/core/include/numpy/npy_common.h):

// from numpy/core/src/common/npy_totorder.c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "numpy/npy_common.h"

static inline uint64_t flip_double_bits(uint64_t x) {
    /* IEEE-754 totalOrder bit-twiddle: flip the sign-bit, then
       if it was negative, invert all bits. This maps double to
       an unsigned integer whose native < is the totalOrder. */
    uint64_t mask = (uint64_t)1 << 63;
    if (x & mask) {
        return ~x;
    }
    return x ^ mask;
}

bool npy_total_order_double(double a, double b) {
    uint64_t ai, bi;
    memcpy(&ai, &a, sizeof(ai));
    memcpy(&bi, &b, sizeof(bi));
    ai = flip_double_bits(ai);
    bi = flip_double_bits(bi);
    return ai < bi;
}

bool npy_total_order_float(float a, float b) {
    uint32_t ai, bi;
    memcpy(&ai, &a, sizeof(ai));
    memcpy(&bi, &b, sizeof(bi));
    /* same flipping trick on 32 bits */
    uint32_t mask = (uint32_t)1 << 31;
    ai = (ai & mask) ? ~ai : (ai ^ mask);
    bi = (bi & mask) ? ~bi : (bi ^ mask);
    return ai < bi;
}

/* For complex: first compare real parts, then imag parts */
bool npy_total_order_cdouble(npy_cdouble a, npy_cdouble b) {
    if (npy_total_order_double(a.real, b.real)) {
        return true;
    }
    if (npy_total_order_double(b.real, a.real)) {
        return false;
    }
    return npy_total_order_double(a.imag, b.imag);
}


To wire this up:

1. Declare prototypes in numpy/core/include/numpy/npy_common.h alongside other predicates.

2. Add basic pytest tests in numpy/core/tests/test_totalorder.py verifying:

- –0 < +0

- Negative NaNs come before all finite values

- Positive NaNs come after all finite values

- Complex ordering branches on real then imag.

3. Optionally expose a Python-level ufunc or helper in numpy.lib for users.

This would give all downstream code a single, correct, fast path for IEEE totalOrder comparisons without re-implementing bit hacks each time, and would align NumPy with Rust’s total_cmp, C++20’s std::strong_order, etc.

