I spent some time exploring the `einsum` implementation and tried a fix based on your suggestion of special-casing the first reduction.

What I tried:
At the point where the output array is initialized inside `PyArray_EinsteinSum` (in `numpy/_core/src/multiarray/einsum.c.src`), I replaced this:
```c
ret = NpyIter_GetOperandArray(iter)[nop];
if (PyArray_AssignZero(ret, NULL) < 0) {
    goto fail;
}
```
with:
```c
ret = NpyIter_GetOperandArray(iter)[nop];
PyObject *identity = NULL;

if (PyArray_DESCR(ret)->type_num == NPY_OBJECT) {
    identity = PyUnicode_FromString("");
} else {
    identity = PyLong_FromLong(0);
}

if (identity == NULL || PyArray_FillWithScalar(ret, identity) < 0) {
    Py_XDECREF(identity);
    goto fail;
}
Py_DECREF(identity);
```
This avoids assuming `0` as the identity for object arrays and instead uses `""` when appropriate. It doesn't look into operand values, just checks the output dtype.

